   "memo": "<ul><li><strong>Subject-oriented [1]:</strong> data is organised around subjects such as student, course, finance and library usage rather than around the applications that captured it.</li><li><strong>Integrated [1]:</strong> consistent naming, encoding, units and formats are enforced across all sources, giving one representation of each entity.</li><li><strong>Time-variant [1]:</strong> every record carries a time reference and historical snapshots are retained over a long horizon.</li><li><strong>Nonvolatile [1]:</strong> users read the data; it is refreshed through controlled ETL cycles rather than updated in place by operational transactions.</li><li><strong>Integration difficulty [1]:</strong> source systems may use different student-number formats, course codes, currencies and date conventions, so cleansing and conforming are required before comparison is possible.</li><li><strong>Time variance and trends [1]:</strong> retaining semester and year snapshots allows cohort comparison, seasonality analysis and long-term trend reporting.</li><li><strong>Nonvolatility and audit [1]:</strong> because history is not overwritten, past reports can be reproduced and decisions can be audited.</li></ul>",
